- quickjs-unary-not-overload-abort: applying unary `~` (bitwise NOT) to *any*
  object whose [Symbol.operatorSet] resolves to a non-primitive operator set
  crashes the whole process with abort() - not a catchable JS exception -
  regardless of whether that operator set defines a `~` handler at all. Root
  cause is in the vendored ../quickjs/quickjs.c (a separate project/repo this
  one builds against, not qjs-modules' own code): js_not_slow()
  (quickjs.c:12730) calls js_call_unary_op_fallback() (quickjs.c:12421) for
  every OP_not, which unconditionally calls get_ovop_from_opcode(OP_not)
  (quickjs.c:12189-12214) before ever checking whether the operator set has
  a `~` entry - and that function's switch has no `case OP_not:` at all, so
  it falls through to `default: abort();`. doc/operator-overloading.md's own
  operator table lists `~` / `JS_OVOP_NOT` as a supported overloadable
  unary operator ("bitwise NOT") - that entry is simply wrong for this
  vendored quickjs.c, at least as currently built.
  Found while trying to wire lib/parser.js's unary `~` up to a Kleene-star
  combinator (ZeroOrMore) - confirmed not specific to Rule/lib/parser.js by
  reproducing the same crash against an unrelated Predicate instance (from
  quickjs-predicate.c, already shipped) with no `~` handler defined for it
  either. This means *any* class in this codebase that installs a
  Symbol.operatorSet (Predicate, and now Rule/Terminal/OneOrMore/Optional/
  Sequence/ZeroOrMore in lib/parser.js) is already exposed to this crash the
  moment user code applies unary `~` to an instance of it - not limited to
  code that deliberately tries to overload `~` itself. Left unfixed: the fix
  belongs in ../quickjs/quickjs.c (add a `case OP_not: return JS_OVOP_NOT;`
  to get_ovop_from_opcode(), matching the JS_OVOP_NOT enum value that
  already exists at quickjs.c:708 and is already listed in
  js_overloadable_operator_names - the pieces are all there except this one
  switch case), which is out of scope for qjs-modules itself.

    import { Predicate } from 'predicate';
    const p = Predicate.type(0);
    ~p;
    // expected: TypeError (no overloaded operator ~) or 0/-1 (no-op numeric
    // bitwise-not fallback), since Predicate's operator set has no '~' entry
    // actual: process aborts (SIGABRT), no JS-catchable exception at all

- serial-default-export-broken: js_serial_init (quickjs-serial.c:669-672)
  gates JS_SetModuleExport(ctx, m, "default", serial_ctor) behind
  `!strcmp(module_name, "cookie")` - "cookie" isn't this module's name under
  any registration in this codebase (looks copy-pasted from an unrelated
  module), so the condition is always false. Worse, JS_INIT_MODULE
  (quickjs-serial.c:687-694) never calls JS_AddModuleExport(ctx, m,
  "default") at all, unlike every other module with a default export
  (quickjs-directory.c:499/519, quickjs-deep.c, quickjs-xml.c, etc., which
  declare+set "default" unconditionally) - so even fixing the name check
  wouldn't be enough, the export slot itself is never declared. Found while
  fixing the analogous sockets-default-export-undeclared bug in
  quickjs-sockets.c, which had the same shape.

    import Serial from 'serial';
    console.log(Serial);
    // expected: the Serial constructor
    // actual: SyntaxError: Could not find export 'default' in module 'serial'

- test-path-stream-preexisting-failures: tests/test_path.js's `skipSeparator()`,
  `isSeparator()`, `isin()`, `equal()`, `toArray()` tests fail, and
  `equal()`/`toArray()` additionally throw "not a function" (suggesting a
  path.js API those tests expect no longer exists under that name);
  tests/test_stream.js's `tee(): both branches independently observe every
  chunk` test also throws "not a function". These assertion failures were
  always there, just never surfaced via exit code until tinytest.js's run()
  (exported as `tests`) was fixed to call std.exit(1) on any test failure
  (tests/tinytest.js:68-75) - before that, every consumer called
  `tests({...})` fire-and-forget at module scope without awaiting the
  returned promise, and run() itself never propagated failure past its own
  console output, so the qjsm process always exited 0 regardless of how many
  assertions failed, silently passing `ctest`. Left unfixed: out of scope
  (neither path.js/stream.js nor their tests have been touched to investigate
  the "not a function" root cause).

    ctest --test-dir build/<arch> -R 'test_path|test_stream'
    # expected: both FAIL, matching the failures their own console output
    # already reports
    # actual: both FAIL (ctest correctly surfaces this now - previously both
    # reported PASSED despite printing "N out of 41 tests failed" to stdout)

- quickjs-stack-size-flag-ineffective: qjsm's `--stack-size n` CLI option (and
  the underlying JS_SetMaxStackSize(), quickjs.c:2408-2412) has no observable
  effect on how deep JS recursion can actually go before "stack overflow" is
  thrown - the effective limit is a constant ~164 nested calls of a trivial
  `function f(n){ return n<=0 ? 0 : 1+f(n-1); }`, identical whether
  `--stack-size` is omitted, set to 10000, or set to 500000000. Binary-searched
  the exact threshold at several flag values (10000, 20000, 50000, 256000,
  5000000, 500000000, and unset) and got 164 every time - ruling out
  under/overflow of the strtod() parse or a too-small value by accident.
  update_stack_limit() (quickjs.c:2399-2406) recomputes rt->stack_limit from
  the *already-captured* rt->stack_top and the new rt->stack_size, which looks
  correct on paper, so either js_check_stack_overflow() (quickjs.c:1648-1664)
  isn't actually gating the interpreter's function-call path the way it
  appears to, or something else in this build (debug/sanitizer flags? a much
  larger per-JS_CallInternal-frame C stack footprint than usual?) is causing a
  real C-level stack exhaustion (caught via SIGSEGV/guard page and reported as
  a catchable "stack overflow", rather than js_check_stack_overflow's own
  proactive check firing first) well before rt->stack_limit would ever be
  reached - 8MB (`ulimit -s`) / 164 frames implies roughly 50KB of C stack per
  JS call frame, which is abnormally large for a single trivial recursive
  function call. Found while running lib/parser.js's Rule-based recursive-
  descent matching against a grammar compiled from tests/Shell-Grammar.y
  (tests/test_grammar.js) - a moderately nested grammar (if/then/else,
  ~15 chained non-terminals deep, tried 3x for condition/then-branch/else-
  branch) hits this same ~164-frame ceiling and throws "stack overflow" with
  no way to raise it via the documented `--stack-size` flag. Worked around in
  tests/test_grammar.js by using a shallower fantasy script (a pipeline and a
  `;`-separated command list, not an if/then/else) that stays within the
  ~164-frame budget; left unfixed here since the actual defect (if it's not
  simply an artifact of this build's compiler flags) is in the vendored
  ../quickjs/quickjs.c, out of scope for qjs-modules itself.

    function f(n) { return n <= 0 ? 0 : 1 + f(n - 1); }
    console.log(f(150));                 // 150, fine
    console.log(f(200));                 // expected: 200 (well under 256KB
                                          // default/any --stack-size budget)
                                          // actual: uncaught "stack overflow"
                                          // - and passing e.g.
                                          // `--stack-size 500000000` first
                                          // changes nothing

- wasm-node-glue-fetch-crash: qjsm.wasm's Emscripten-generated glue
  (build/wasm32-unknown-emscripten/qjsm.js, emitted by emsdk's bundled
  emscripten 2.0.10) picks the wasm-loading path in `instantiateAsync()`
  by checking only `typeof fetch === 'function'`, with no
  `ENVIRONMENT_IS_NODE` guard (qjsm.js:1534-1554). Node 18+ ships a global
  `fetch`, so under any modern Node this branch runs `fetch(wasmBinaryFile,
  ...)` with a plain filesystem path instead of the Node-native
  `readFileSync` path a few lines below, and `fetch()` throws immediately
  because the argument isn't a URL. This is a known compatibility gap
  between old Emscripten releases and Node's newer global fetch, fixed in
  more recent Emscripten by adding the environment check; upgrading emsdk
  (or patching the generated glue) would fix it properly. Found while
  smoke-testing qjsm.wasm after enabling QUICKJS_INTERNAL for the wasm
  build. Workaround below unblocks manual testing but isn't a real fix.

    node -e "delete globalThis.fetch;
             process.argv=[process.argv[0],'qjsm.js','-e','console.log(1)'];
             require('./qjsm.js')"
    // without the `delete globalThis.fetch`: TypeError: Failed to parse
    // URL from qjsm.wasm
    // with it: prints 1 as expected

- wasm-qjsm-no-real-file-imports: qjsm.wasm has no filesystem wired up to
  the host when run under Node (`-sNODERAWFS` was never set, see
  ../CMakeLists.txt:43-68, and no `--preload-file`/NODEFS mount is done
  either) -- only `MEMFS`, which starts empty. Every native/JS builtin
  module registered via BUILTINS_NATIVE/BUILTINS_COMPILED loads fine
  (`--list` shows all 27 native + 14 compiled-JS modules, and `-e` can
  import and use them), but `-m <realfile.js>` for any file that actually
  lives on disk fails because that file was never copied into MEMFS. This
  means qjsm.wasm today can only run inline `-e` scripts (or scripts naming
  a builtin module by name) under Node, not arbitrary local .js files --
  the primary way qjs-modules' own lib/*.js wrappers are normally loaded.
  Found in the same wasm smoke-test session as the entry above.

    // From build/wasm32-unknown-emscripten, with a file test.js sitting
    // right next to qjsm.js/qjsm.wasm:
    node -e "delete globalThis.fetch;
             process.argv=[process.argv[0],'qjsm.js','-m','test.js'];
             require('./qjsm.js')"
    // expected: test.js runs
    // actual: ReferenceError: could not load module 'test.js': ReferenceError:
    //         could not load module filename 'test.js'

- missing-line-number-single-line-function: a stack frame for a function whose
  entire body (declaration through the throwing statement) sits on one
  source line prints with no `:<line>` at all - just the bare filename -
  while every enclosing/enclosed frame on its own line gets a correct line
  number. Root cause is in the vendored ../quickjs/quickjs.c (not
  qjs-modules' own code): build_backtrace() (quickjs.c:6497) calls
  find_line_num(ctx, b, sf->cur_pc - b->byte_code_buf - 1) for each stack
  frame and only appends `:%d` `if(line_num1 != -1)` (quickjs.c:6553); for a
  one-line function body find_line_num() apparently can't resolve a line for
  that pc and returns -1, silently dropping the position instead of falling
  back to the function's declaration line. Splitting the exact same code
  across multiple lines produces a fully located trace. Found via
  tests/test_exceptions.js's 'synchronous throw inside a single-line
  function body' case, which documents this as a known-failing test (same
  convention as the pre-existing test_path.js/test_stream.js failures).

    // tests/test_exceptions.js spawns qjsm on this exact script:
    function foo() { throw new Error("boom"); }
    function bar() { foo(); }
    bar();
    // actual stack (foo/bar frames have no line number):
    //   at foo (script.js)
    //   at bar (script.js)
    //   at <anonymous> (script.js:3)
    // expected: every frame carries a `:<line>`, e.g. "at foo (script.js:1)"

- js-module-loader-detection-vs-actual-headers (CMakeLists.txt:544, cmake/QuickJSModule.cmake:4):
  the configure-time probe that decides whether qjsm.c should call the 3-arg
  or 4-arg js_module_loader() compiles its test snippet against
  ${QUICKJS_SOURCES_ROOT} (the sibling ../quickjs checkout, which this
  monorepo edits/rebuilds continuously) via compile_code()'s
  "-DINCLUDE_DIRECTORIES=${QUICKJS_SOURCES_ROOT}", but the real qjsm.c
  translation units are compiled with "-I/usr/local/include/quickjs" (the
  separately installed, not-necessarily-in-sync system headers). when the
  sibling source's js_module_loader() signature has been edited but
  /usr/local hasn't been reinstalled yet, the probe result no longer
  matches what qjsm.c actually sees, so JS_MODULE_LOADER_OLD gets set
  wrong for the real build - this can either fail the build outright
  ("too many arguments to function js_module_loader") or, worse, compile
  cleanly with a mismatched signature. same root pattern as the
  QUICKJS_INTERNAL segfault entry below: probes/generation steps trust the
  live sibling source tree while actual compilation/linking trusts the
  separately installed /usr/local quickjs, and nothing checks the two
  agree.

    // rm -rf build/x86_64-linux-gnu && cmake -B build/x86_64-linux-gnu -S .
    // && cmake --build build/x86_64-linux-gnu --target qjsm
    // actual (intermittent, depends on how ../quickjs/quickjs-libc.h
    // currently reads vs. what's installed under /usr/local/include/quickjs):
    //   error: too many arguments to function 'js_module_loader'
    // expected: consistent, correct macro selection every configure

- qjsm-quickjs-internal-struct-drift-segfault (src/qjsm.c:457 jsm_builtin_init,
  quickjs-internal.c): when qjs-modules is built with QUICKJS_INTERNAL=1 (the
  default whenever a sibling ../quickjs/quickjs.c is found), CMakeLists.txt
  generates quickjs-internal.h by sed-extracting internal struct layouts
  (JSModuleDef, JSFunctionBytecode, etc.) out of ${QUICKJS_SOURCES_ROOT}/quickjs.c
  at configure time. qjsm.c and quickjs-internal.c then use those offsets to
  poke JSModuleDef internals directly (module_rename(), module_exports_get(),
  js_module_at()). if the currently-installed /usr/local/lib/.../libquickjs.so.1.1
  was built from a *different* revision of quickjs.c than the one the struct
  layout was snapshotted from (routine in this monorepo, since many sibling
  projects rebuild+reinstall the shared quickjs core), the offsets silently
  desync from the real ABI - there is no version/ABI check anywhere in this
  path. confirmed via valgrind: loading the native "inspect" module (pulled
  in transitively by lib/console.js's `import inspect from 'inspect'`)
  produces a corrupted JSModuleDef/function-bytecode object, later
  dereferenced as a bogus pointer (observed at address 0x82) inside
  libquickjs.so.1.1's js_create_module_function(), segfaulting the process
  before any user script code runs. reproduced with a stale build dir
  (build/x86_64-linux-gnu) and with the then-current /usr/local/bin/qjsm;
  confirmed fixed only after a full `rm -rf build/x86_64-linux-gnu` +
  reconfigure + rebuild (a `--target clean` rebuild in place was NOT
  sufficient - see the js-module-loader-detection-vs-actual-headers entry
  above for why a plain reconfigure of a long-lived shared build dir can
  still land on the wrong macros/headers).

    // env QUICKJS_MODULE_PATH="build/x86_64-linux-debug;." qjsm -e "std.exit(0)"
    // actual: Segmentation fault (confirmed via valgrind: invalid read at a
    //         bogus small address inside js_create_module_function, called
    //         from jsm_module_loader while loading the "inspect" module)
    // expected: script runs (or, at worst, a clean JS-catchable error - not
    //           a segfault before user code executes)

- process-js-scriptargs-undefined-under-dash-e (lib/process.js:18,62): once the
  qjsm-quickjs-internal-struct-drift-segfault entry above is fixed, `qjsm -e
  "<expr>"` still throws an unhandled ReferenceError while initializing the
  "process" builtin module. scriptIndex()'s default parameter `a = scriptArgs`
  (line 18) and the `argv`/`argv0`/`execArgv` properties (lines 60-62)
  reference the global `scriptArgs`, but qjsm only defines that global for
  file/script execution, not for `-e` expression evaluation - so the IIFE
  building `execArgv` (line 62) throws immediately when the process module's
  exports are constructed. because this happens inside a module
  initialization promise, it surfaces as an "unhandled promise rejection"
  (per the recent "always track rejected promises" change) rather than as a
  normal catchable exception, and leaves `globalThis.std`/`globalThis.process`
  etc. never assigned - so any expression that follows and uses `std` (e.g.
  `std.exit(0)`) then fails with a second, unrelated-looking
  "ReferenceError: 'std' is not defined".

    // qjsm -e "std.exit(0)"
    // actual: Possibly unhandled promise rejection: ReferenceError:
    //         'scriptArgs' is not defined (lib/process.js:18), followed by
    //         Error evaluating expression: ReferenceError: 'std' is not
    //         defined
    // expected: std.exit(0) runs and exits cleanly

- quickjs-internal-h-design (CMakeLists.txt:909-969, quickjs-internal.c,
  include/utils.h): the whole QUICKJS_INTERNAL feature - not just the
  configure-time drift covered by qjsm-quickjs-internal-struct-drift-segfault
  above - is bad design and should be untangled/removed. summary of what
  depends on it and why, for whoever does that work:

  generation: when a sibling ../quickjs/quickjs.c is found next to this repo,
  CMakeLists.txt (909-920) force-enables QUICKJS_INTERNAL and (939-969) runs
  a `sed` one-liner over ${QUICKJS_SOURCES_ROOT}/quickjs.c and quickjs-libc.c
  at *configure* time, scraping out struct/enum/typedef definitions by
  pattern-matching brace-balanced blocks, and writes the result verbatim into
  build/.../quickjs-internal.h. there is no parser here, just line-oriented
  sed heuristics against whatever the sibling source currently looks like -
  fragile by construction, and the reason the segfault above was possible at
  all (the snapshot silently goes stale the moment ../quickjs/quickjs.c
  changes and this project isn't reconfigured, or is reconfigured but not
  cleanly rebuilt).

  direct includers of quickjs-internal.h: only quickjs-internal.c itself and
  src/qjsm.c.

  everything else reaches its ~30 exported symbols (module_name,
  module_nameval, module_namecstr, module_ns, module_exports_get,
  module_imports, module_reqmodules, module_rename, module_next/prev/last,
  module_indexof, js_module_at, js_module_find_fwd/rev, js_modules_entries,
  js_modules_object, js_cstring_ptr/dup/len/value, js_get_bytecode,
  js_opcode_list/array/object, js_sab_alloc/free/dup, js_std_file) only via
  prototypes unconditionally declared in include/utils.h (no QUICKJS_INTERNAL
  guard there) - so every call site elsewhere has to guard itself with
  `#if QUICKJS_INTERNAL` or the link fails when it's off. that split (header
  declares unconditionally, only the .c defines conditionally, every caller
  re-derives the same guard) is itself a design smell: the "is this feature
  available" decision is duplicated at every call site instead of living in
  one place.

  who actually uses it, and for what:
    - src/qjsm.c (jsm_builtin_init, jsm_module_load, jsm_module_loader,
      js_eval_buf): renames/looks up/copies-exports-of builtin modules while
      bootstrapping (module_rename, js_module_at, module_exports_get,
      module_next/last) - this is on the qjsm startup path, i.e. exactly
      the code that segfaulted.
    - src/qjsm.c (~1580-1820): implements a JS-visible `Module` reflection
      object (moduleLoader/findModule/loadModule/addModule/resolveModule/
      getModuleName/getModuleExports/getModuleImports/getModuleNamespace/
      getModuleFunction/... CFUNC table) - a debug/introspection API with no
      other implementation path than poking JSModuleDef directly.
    - quickjs-misc.c (~2018-2090, 3574-3798): misc.pointer()/misc.length()
      fast-path over raw JSString internals (js_cstring_ptr), better error
      messages naming the module that failed to resolve, and
      getOpcodes()/getBytecode() raw bytecode/opcode introspection.
    - quickjs-inspect.c (~1353): console/util.inspect() prints a `[module
      '<name>']` label for JS_TAG_MODULE values - falls back to an unnamed
      `[module '']` when QUICKJS_INTERNAL is off, so this one degrades
      gracefully.
    - src/utils.c (~2497-2620, 3395): module_exports_find_str/module_exports/
      js_modules_map/module_value/module_entry/js_module_def/
      js_module_find_from/js_module_find - helper glue shared by the above,
      also used by src/utils.c:3395's std/os module-getter fallback path.

  why this is worth untangling: this is a large, security/stability-relevant
  surface (direct struct pokes into a JS engine's internal module/bytecode
  representation) bought entirely to implement a handful of introspection
  conveniences (module listing/renaming/name-lookup, raw bytecode dumping,
  a console.log label), gated behind a feature flag that CMake silently
  force-enables whenever it happens to find a sibling checkout, generated by
  a sed script with no versioning or ABI check against whatever
  libquickjs.so is actually linked. every caller re-implements its own
  `#if QUICKJS_INTERNAL` fallback, so the "public API" surface of this
  project quietly changes shape depending on an environment detail the user
  never asked about. untangling should mean: either upstream the small set
  of genuinely missing QuickJS C-API entry points this needs (module name/
  exports/list-by-index accessors are reasonable public API asks) and drop
  quickjs-internal.h entirely, or at minimum stop auto-enabling it from
  CMake and require an explicit, documented opt-in with a real ABI/version
  check instead of blind struct-offset trust.

